[ Jews ](/wiki/Jews "Jews") , [ Poles ](/wiki/Polish_people "Polish people") ,
and [ Roma ](/wiki/Romani_people "Romani people") were subjected to [ genocide
](/wiki/Genocide "Genocide") as "undesirable" racial groups in [ The Holocaust
](/wiki/The_Holocaust "The Holocaust") . The Nazis established [ ghettos
](/wiki/Ghettos_in_occupied_Europe_1939%E2%80%931944 "Ghettos in occupied
Europe 1939–1944") in order to confine Jews and sometimes, they confined Romas
in tightly packed areas of the cities of [ Eastern Europe
](/wiki/Eastern_Europe "Eastern Europe") , turning them into _[ de facto
](/wiki/De_facto "De facto") _ [ concentration camps
](/wiki/Concentration_camp "Concentration camp") . The [ Warsaw Ghetto
](/wiki/Warsaw_Ghetto "Warsaw Ghetto") was the largest of these ghettos, with
400,000 people. The [ Łódź Ghetto ](/wiki/%C5%81%C3%B3d%C5%BA_Ghetto "Łódź
Ghetto") was the second largest, holding about 160,000.  [46]

