Popular frequency domain algorithms include: the [ harmonic product spectrum
](/w/index.php?title=Harmonic_product_spectrum&action=edit&redlink=1 "Harmonic
product spectrum \(page does not exist\)") ;  [5]  [6]  [ cepstral
](/wiki/Cepstrum "Cepstrum") analysis  [7]  and [ maximum likelihood
](/wiki/Maximum_likelihood "Maximum likelihood") which attempts to match the
frequency domain characteristics to pre-defined frequency maps (useful for
detecting pitch of fixed tuning instruments); and the detection of peaks due
to harmonic series.  [8]

A **pitch detection algorithm** ( **PDA** ) is an [ algorithm
](/wiki/Algorithm "Algorithm") designed to estimate the [ pitch
](/wiki/Pitch_\(music\) "Pitch \(music\)") or [ fundamental frequency
](/wiki/Fundamental_frequency "Fundamental frequency") of a [ quasiperiodic
](/wiki/Quasiperiodic "Quasiperiodic") or [ oscillating ](/wiki/Oscillation
"Oscillation") signal, usually a [ digital recording ](/wiki/Digital_recording
"Digital recording") of [ speech ](/wiki/Speech_processing "Speech
processing") or a musical note or tone. This can be done in the [ time domain
](/wiki/Time_domain "Time domain") , the [ frequency domain
](/wiki/Frequency_domain "Frequency domain") , or both.

