  Overview  [  [ edit
](/w/index.php?title=Reward_system&action=edit&section=3 "Edit section:
Overview") ]

The brain structures that compose the reward system are located primarily
within the [ cortico-basal ganglia-thalamo-cortical loop ](/wiki/Cortico-
basal_ganglia-thalamo-cortical_loop "Cortico-basal ganglia-thalamo-cortical
loop") ;  [13]  the [ basal ganglia ](/wiki/Basal_ganglia "Basal ganglia")
portion of the loop drives activity within the reward system.  [13]  Most of
the pathways that connect structures within the reward system are [
glutamatergic ](/wiki/Glutamatergic "Glutamatergic") [ interneurons
](/wiki/Interneuron "Interneuron") , [ GABAergic ](/wiki/GABAergic
"GABAergic") [ medium spiny neurons ](/wiki/Medium_spiny_neuron "Medium spiny
neuron") (MSNs), and [ dopaminergic ](/wiki/Dopaminergic "Dopaminergic") [
projection neurons ](/wiki/Projection_neuron "Projection neuron") ,  [13]
[14]  although other types of projection neurons contribute (e.g., [
orexinergic ](/wiki/Orexinergic "Orexinergic") projection neurons). The reward
system includes the [ ventral tegmental area ](/wiki/Ventral_tegmental_area
"Ventral tegmental area") , [ ventral striatum ](/wiki/Ventral_striatum
"Ventral striatum") (i.e., the [ nucleus accumbens ](/wiki/Nucleus_accumbens
"Nucleus accumbens") and [ olfactory tubercle ](/wiki/Olfactory_tubercle
"Olfactory tubercle") ), [ dorsal striatum ](/wiki/Dorsal_striatum "Dorsal
striatum") (i.e., the [ caudate nucleus ](/wiki/Caudate_nucleus "Caudate
nucleus") and [ putamen ](/wiki/Putamen "Putamen") ), [ substantia nigra
](/wiki/Substantia_nigra "Substantia nigra") (i.e., the [ pars compacta
](/wiki/Pars_compacta "Pars compacta") and [ pars reticulata
](/wiki/Pars_reticulata "Pars reticulata") ), [ prefrontal cortex
](/wiki/Prefrontal_cortex "Prefrontal cortex") , [ anterior cingulate cortex
](/wiki/Anterior_cingulate_cortex "Anterior cingulate cortex") , [ insular
cortex ](/wiki/Insular_cortex "Insular cortex") , [ hippocampus
](/wiki/Hippocampus "Hippocampus") , [ hypothalamus ](/wiki/Hypothalamus
"Hypothalamus") (particularly, the [ orexinergic ](/wiki/Orexinergic
"Orexinergic") nucleus in the [ lateral hypothalamus
](/wiki/Lateral_hypothalamus "Lateral hypothalamus") ), [ thalamus
](/wiki/Thalamus "Thalamus") (multiple nuclei), [ subthalamic nucleus
](/wiki/Subthalamic_nucleus "Subthalamic nucleus") , [ globus pallidus
](/wiki/Globus_pallidus "Globus pallidus") (both [ external
](/wiki/Globus_pallidus_external "Globus pallidus external") and [ internal
](/wiki/Globus_pallidus_internal "Globus pallidus internal") ), [ ventral
pallidum ](/wiki/Ventral_pallidum "Ventral pallidum") , [ parabrachial nucleus
](/wiki/Parabrachial_nucleus "Parabrachial nucleus") , [ amygdala
](/wiki/Amygdala "Amygdala") , and the remainder of the [ extended amygdala
](/wiki/Extended_amygdala "Extended amygdala") .  [2]  [13]  [15]  [16]  [17]
The [ dorsal raphe nucleus ](/wiki/Dorsal_raphe_nucleus "Dorsal raphe
nucleus") and [ cerebellum ](/wiki/Cerebellum "Cerebellum") appear to modulate
some forms of reward-related cognition (i.e., [ associative learning
](/wiki/Associative_learning "Associative learning") , [ motivational salience
](/wiki/Motivational_salience "Motivational salience") , and [ positive
emotions ](/wiki/Positive_affectivity "Positive affectivity") ) and behaviors
as well.  [18]  [19]  [20]  The [ laterodorsal tegmental nucleus (LDT)
](/wiki/Laterodorsal_tegmental_nucleus "Laterodorsal tegmental nucleus") , [
pedunculopontine nucleus (PPTg) ](/wiki/Pedunculopontine_nucleus
"Pedunculopontine nucleus") , and [ lateral habenula (LHb) ](/wiki/Habenula
"Habenula") (both directly and indirectly via the [ rostromedial tegmental
nucleus (RMTg) ](/wiki/Rostromedial_tegmental_nucleus "Rostromedial tegmental
nucleus") ) are also capable of inducing [ aversive salience
](/wiki/Aversive_salience "Aversive salience") and [ incentive salience
](/wiki/Incentive_salience "Incentive salience") through their projections to
the ventral tegmental area (VTA).  [21]  The LDT and PPTg both send
